.cs 1 on
Overview of CMS Make

CMS Make attempts to follow a minimal subset of POSIX 'make' features.
The overriding goal is that you can have a common makefile between CMS
and various Unix or POSIX environments (eg: Linux or OpenVM).

The syntax used by CMS Make is slightly different from the official
rules syntax for POSIX 'make' rules file or "makefile".  But it is
visually similar, easier to use in CMS (with XEDIT), and iscompatible
with common practice in makefiles.
.cs 1 off

.cs 2 on
¢|Rules Syntax¢%

A simple rule in CMS Make looks like ...
 +-------------+-------------------------------------------------------+
 |   target:   |   dependency   [dependency  ...]                      |
 |             |   action                                              |
 |             |   [action]                                            |
 |             |   [...]                                               |
 +-------------+-------------------------------------------------------+

The "target" is a file.  The "dependency" is a file (or files).
The action is what to do if the dependency is newer than the target
or if the dependency exists and the target does not.

Actions must be indented,  but the leading white space may be blanks
and need not be TAB characters as with POSIX 'make' rules syntax.


.cs 2 off



CMS MAKE DOES NOT HANDLE COMPLEX QUOTING.
Commands read from the rules file(s) are passed to CP, CMS,
or CMS Pipelines and are not passed to a shell unless explicitly
indicitated.  (Which also requires that the shell be installed
which is beyond the scope of this document.)


